  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 244 PM EDT FRI OCT 30 2026\n\nAfter roughly two weeks of dry weather, rain should finally make a \ncomeback during the long term period. The question is when. What at \none time looked like a good bet for damp weather returning on Sunday \nis continuing to look less likely, with a greater probability for \nrain now waiting until later in the new week.\n\nThe start of the period will see a large but meager-for-its-size \nshortwave trough rippling through what would otherwise be a sizable \nupper ridge over most of the eastern CONUS. The trough axis should \nbe laid out roughly from IA to the FL panhandle Sunday morning and \nwill pass eastward over the JKL forecast area Sunday night and early \nMonday. At the surface, high pressure centered along or offshore of \nthe Mid Atlantic coast will ridge westward along the \ncentral/southern Appalachians, with a cold air damming scenario \nsetting up in the flow against the eastern side of the mountains and \ndownsloping here. Along with downsloping, our low level flow will \nstill be largely originating out of the high, with moisture \nadvection off the Atlantic and gulf wrapping around us to the south \nand west early in the week. Thus, as the initial upper trough passes \nover, our moisture should still be limited in the lower levels. Will \nlook for mid-high clouds and virga to be abundant, but it is \nquestionable how much if any precip will be realized. The GFS has \ntrended toward the more consistently drier ECMWF for the early \nweek wave, and our POP is now dropping further in this forecast \ncycle, as deterministic runs of all models are now mainly dry. \nThe western and northwestern portions of our area, furthest \nremoved from downsloping and closest to the moisture channel, \nwould have the best shot at rain.\n\nBy the time our early week trough weakens and departs to our east \nMonday night, another stronger long wave trough should be \nprogressing eastward across the central CONUS, accompanied by a deep \nlow, or series of lows. Models do not quite agree on the evolution \nof this system late in the week as it affects our area. The ECMWF is \ndeeper with the upper trough and slower to close off an upper low \ncompared to the GFS. However, they both eventually close off an \nupper low and track it nearby at about the same time frame, with \nanother large surface low deepening significantly as it passes. The \nstronger ECMWF solution results in colder air in the system's wake \nby Friday. At this stage, the result of latest developments is an \nincrease in POP with the system in the Wednesday to Thursday time \nframe, and low confidence in the temperature forecast by Friday.",
